            "url": "https://www.politico.com/story/2019/02/13/china-trump-xi-trade-talks-1165792",
            "title": "China aims to run out the clock to get Trump and Xi to the table ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# China aims to run out the clock to get Trump and Xi to the table on trade\nLighthizer continues to press China to meet a list of demands that includes major structural changes to its state-run economy. That includes changing policies and regulations the U.S. accuses of coercing U.S. firms to hand over valuable technology as a condition of doing business and addressing a web of national and provincial subsidy programs that disadvantage foreign competitors.\n\nBut Trump’s actions and words, however, are sending signals to Chinese officials that they might be able to get away with reverting to their playbook of making minor concessions",
              "One source who declined to be named because of the sensitive nature of the negotiations said China has only made offers in line with Xi’s speech at last April’s Boao Forum.\n\nAt the time, he made commitments to ease restrictions on foreign banks and insurance companies, lower barriers on auto imports, and increase enforcement of intellectual property. Xi also reiterated commitments to foster a “world-class business environment” during a speech in November at the China International Import Expo",
              "Heavier demands, such as addressing tech transfer and subsidy policies, could take years to carry out, even if Beijing was in a mindset to do so. In the meantime, negotiators have less than 20 days to meet a March 1 deadline to reach some kind of a deal, or at least a ceasefire, before the U.S. escalates tariffs on $200 billion worth of goods. That number represents around 40 percent of items the U.S. imports from China",
              "A key part of the deal will be holding China to any commitments it makes. Lighthizer is working to develop an enforcement mechanism that could include re-imposition of tariffs and the revival of a now-lapsed safeguard measure to stop surges of Chinese imports that Beijing agreed to when it joined the World Trade Organization, according to two sources close to the discussions. He’s also expressed reluctance at rolling back tariffs beyond refraining from any additional escalation, those source said",
              "“They need to negotiate from a position of high leverage because they know the Chinese want to do the absolute bare minimum to satisfy the administration,” said one source.\n\nOn Tuesday, Trump appeared to soften his administration’s previously hardened stance, saying the deadline could be extended if the two sides appear close to a deal. The president acknowledged last week that a meeting with Xi was unlikely to occur before March 1",
              "“They’re going to spend, we hope, considerable amounts on time on areas where they’ve made much less progress, particularly those around forced technology transfer, industrial policy issues, and the enforcement mechanisms of a potential agreement,” said Jeremie Waterman, vice president for greater China at the U.S. Chamber of Commerce",
              "On one front, the president has stuck to punishing China for past actions. Trump is expected to sign an executive order as soon as this week banning the use of Chinese telecommunication equipment made by Huawei and ZTE on U.S. wireless networks for national security reasons.\n\nThere’s also the question of what China will get in exchange for making any concessions, whether it’s more commodity purchases or the structural changes the U.S. is demanding"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "http://www.xinhuanet.com/english/2019-02/02/c_137794379.htm",
            "title": "Xinhua Headlines: China, U.S. make important progress in trade ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# Xinhua Headlines: China, U.S. make important progress in trade talks, paving way for further consultations\nDuring the two-day talks, the two sides discussed the topics of trade balance, technology transfer, protection of intellectual property rights (IPR), non-tariff barriers, service sector, agriculture and enforcement mechanism, as well as certain issues of particular concern for the Chinese side.\n\nImportant progress has been made for the current stage, according to the Chinese delegation",
              "The two sides have agreed to take effective measures to promote a more balanced development of bilateral trade. The Chinese side will make active efforts to expand imports from the United States in the sectors of agriculture, energy, manufacturing and services, which will help China's pursuit of high-quality economic development and meet the people's demand for a better life.\n\nThe two sides also discussed some specific concerns of the Chinese side, and the U.S. side said it would seriously address these concerns",
              "Both sides believe that it is very important to establish an effective two-way enforcement mechanism, so as to ensure all measures agreed upon through consultations will be implemented. They have reached consensus in principle on the framework and basic elements of the enforcement mechanism, and will continue to hammer out more details.\n\n**CRITICAL STAGE**\n\nWhen meeting with Trump, Liu delivered a message from Xi, in which the Chinese president pointed out that China-U.S. relations are at a critical stage"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2019/02/02/trad-f02.html",
            "title": "No deal reached at US-China trade meeting - World Socialist Web Site",
            "snippets": [
              "## No deal reached at US-China trade meeting\n##### Nick Beams\n2 February 2019\n\nThe two days of top-level talks between representatives of the Chinese government and the Trump administration on trade held in Washington this week have led to a commitment to hold further discussions but no concrete agreement",
              "The main focus of the talks was not on increasing US exports to China but reducing the trade imbalance between the two countries. China has already agreed to undertake measures such as buying five million tons of soybeans from the US.\n\nThe key issues concerned US demands for what it calls “structural reform” of the Chinese economy, centring on the protection of intellectual property rights, the cessation of forced technology transfers and the winding back of state subsidies for major industries that Washington claims are “market distorting.”",
              "The Chinese negotiators insist they cannot offer anything on that score because “forced” transfers do not take place and that agreements with US firms to make available their technologies form part of commercial deals to gain greater access to the Chinese market.\n\nFollowing the discussions, Lighthizer, who, together with White House economic adviser Peter Navarro, is the main anti-China hawk in the administration, offered a relatively upbeat assessment",
              "While the negotiations have produced no results so far as specific commitments are concerned, they have made clear one of the key demands of the US. It will not accept on its face a signed commitment by the Chinese government or legislative changes regarding issues such as intellectual property. It is insisting that there must be some mechanism established through which the US can directly intervene to assess whether the agreed measures are being carried out.\n\n“If we can get an agreement, it’s worth nothing without enforcement,” Lighthizer said",
              "The *South China Morning Post* reported that Lighthizer had summed up one of the crucial components of the talks as “enforcement, enforcement, enforcement.”\n\nThis raises the crucial question of how such enforcement would be carried out. The US would not hand over that task to an international body such as the World Trade Organization. The Chinese government could not accept the direct intervention of US officials in the operations of its legal system, or scrutiny of government economic decisions—which would amount to an outright violation of its national sovereignty",
              "Whatever the twists and turns in negotiations over the next month, the underlying issue remains the drive of the US to push back China’s economic and above all technological development, which it regards as a threat to both its economic and military hegemony.\n\nAs numerous commentators have pointed out, the measures being undertaken by the Chinese state to promote industrial and technological advancement are similar to those undertaken by other countries in an earlier period"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/12/business/us-china-trade-talks.html?emc=edit_th_190213&amp=&nl=todaysheadlines&amp=&nlid=253946410213",
            "title": "U.S.-China Trade Talks Face Big Obstacle: Ensuring That Promises ...",
            "snippets": [
              "American negotiators, worried that Beijing will drag its feet on sticking to the provisions of a deal, want a way to punish failures to comply. Their Chinese counterparts may not go along.",
              "# U.S.-China Trade Talks Face Big Obstacle: Ensuring That Promises Are Kept\nThe Port of New Orleans. With trade negotiators for the United States and China set to meet this week, how to punish Beijing if it does not meet its obligations will probably be a contentious issue.Credit...Edmund D. Fountain for The New York Times\n\nBy Keith Bradsher\n\nFeb.",
              "BEIJING — When China joined the World Trade Organization, the global fraternity of cross-border commerce, it promised to open itself up to foreigners in lucrative businesses like banking, telecommunications and electronic-payment processing.\n\nMore than 17 years later, China’s telecommunications industry remains firmly under government control. Only recently did China say it would allow foreign companies to own their own bank businesses here. And after nearly two decades of legal fights, China is still reviewing the applications of Visa and Mastercard to get into the country’s payment-processing market",
              "The broken promises hang over Robert Lighthizer, the United States trade representative, and Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin as they arrive in Beijing for two days of talks to end a trade war between the United States and China. They face a March 2 deadline, after which President Trump has threatened to increase by more than double the tariffs the administration imposed last autumn on $200 billion a year on Chinese imports",
              "A top priority for Mr. Lighthizer and Mr. Mnuchin, according to several people with detailed knowledge of the negotiations, is to ensure that any deal they strike has teeth if Beijing does not live up to its obligations.\n\nAmerican negotiators want to create a mechanism that would automatically raise tariffs on Chinese goods if its exports to the United States keep rising, said three of the people, who spoke on the condition of anonymity because the talks are not public",

